"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA solid sphere of ice with radius 4 is placed on a hollow cone (vertex pointed down) of radius 4 and height 5. When the ice melts, how much of the water will overflow the cone? Express your answer as a common fraction in terms of $\\pi$. (For this problem, we ignore the fact that the volume of water decreases when it melts.)","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"003e6571230324f88c6d0b20ab7dd5cdd3096aa2fb25c0056ffdb2dbf406383f"},"seconds":12.645051956176758,"seed":134712304328161225,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to determine the volume of the ice sphere and compare it to the volume of the cone to find out how much water will overflow when the ice melts.\n\n1. **Calculate the volume of the ice sphere:**\n   The formula for the volume of a sphere is given by:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3\n   \\]\n   Here, the radius \\( r \\) of the sphere is 4. So, we substitute \\( r = 4 \\) into the formula:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (4)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 = \\frac{256}{3} \\pi\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the volume of the cone:**\n   The formula for the volume of a cone is given by:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{cone}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n   \\]\n   Here, the radius \\( r \\) of the cone is 4 and the height \\( h \\) is 5. So, we substitute \\( r = 4 \\) and \\( h = 5 \\) into the formula:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{cone}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (4)^2 (5) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 16 \\cdot 5 = \\frac{80}{3} \\pi\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the volume of water that will overflow:**\n   When the ice melts, it will form water that will fill the cone up to its maximum capacity. The volume of the water that will overflow is the volume of the ice sphere minus the volume of the cone:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{overflow}} = V_{\\text{sphere}} - V_{\\text{cone}} = \\frac{256}{3} \\pi - \\frac{80}{3} \\pi = \\frac{256 - 80}{3} \\pi = \\frac{176}{3} \\pi\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the amount of water that will overflow the cone is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{176}{3} \\pi}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{176}{3} \\pi}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:175"}
